70
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
In this week's podcast, let's see, I have an email question to answer.

71
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:10,000
I think it's a pretty good email question

72
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
And I have some thoughts to share with you in Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less,

73
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast.

74
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So let's get started, shall we?

75
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less.

76
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Hey, have you ever taken the time to really examine the vision of your work as a scouter?

77
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Ooh, vision,

78
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
Oh, that's a loaded word, isn't it.

79
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Either you're thinking about, like you know, Carlos Castaneda in the desert,

80
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
or some really interminable corporate team building session

81
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
where we talk about visions and oh man.

82
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
But visions aren't really mystical,

83
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:01,000
They're not difficult to understand.

84
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:05,000
They're not just apparitions we glimpse through the ether somewhere.

85
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
They're not just a fuzzy cloud of inspiration

86
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
And you know they can be all of those things,

87
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:16,000
but they don't really have to be, and they're much more practical and pragmatic than that.

88
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
A vision isn't quite a goal, maybe, but it's hard to tell the difference between the two.

89
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
Basically, what I'm talking about today is the way you picture where you're headed.

90
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
What's the picture in your mind?

91
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:34,000
A vision is really just a picture of a future state that we're working towards,

92
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Kind of like a street view.

93
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
If you're familiar with Google Street View, right,

94
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
If you're headed for a destination you've never been before,

95
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
Street View will show you a picture as if you're standing right in front of that destination.

96
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
It's something I find pretty reassuring.

97
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
If I'm going somewhere I haven't been before, I can look at a picture.

98
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
I say, oh, so this is what it looks like,

99
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
This is the storefront or the house or whatever,

100
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:02,000
And it's very reassuring because when I finally get there, I go: oh okay,

101
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:08,000
I had this envisioned as being looking like this and I know that I'm in the right place.

102
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
So, like I said, a vision is a picture of the future state that you're working towards,

103
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:19,000
Just in the same way that having a picture of the address that you're headed for is kind of reassuring.

104
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Hey, I'm here, I got there.

105
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:28,000
I have something in my mind that is able to help me figure out that I've arrived or I've gotten to the right place.

106
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
So, if we extend the analogy a little bit, Google Street Views-

107
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
they come from vehicles that travel around with cameras.

108
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:39,000
Where do the pictures of our future state come from?

109
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:41,000
They have to come from somewhere.

110
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:47,000
We imagine them based on things that we've seen before, places that we've been before.

111
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:53,000
When I first became a scout master, my vision was defined by what I knew at the time,

112
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
what I had seen, what I had listened to, what I had read,

113
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And one of the things that was like a really, really strong part of that vision

114
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,000
was a very old copy of the Patrol Leaders Handbook

115
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:15,000
from the 1940s that was illustrated with black-and-white kind of cartoonish drawings,

116
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:19,000
And in that handbook everything was perfect, right,

117
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
All the tents lined up in the campsite and the scouts were smiling and cheering

118
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and gathering around their patrol flag.

119
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
They all had impeccable uniforms and everything.

120
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:34,000
So the vision that I had of scouting originally was a very heavily idealized

121
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and sanitized representation of what I actually encountered in working with scouts,

122
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:45,000
And I was frankly disappointed when what I was doing as a scouter didn't look like those drawings.

123
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
The pictures in my head were really, really powerful.

124
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:53,000
It took a long time to realize that's what was driving my ambitions were those pictures.

125
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
I mean, it sounds silly now,

126
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
but I was trying to recreate what I understood and what I knew

127
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
based on my past experiences, without really examining or questioning it.

128
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:09,000
And when things weren't working out to look like those pictures,

129
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I needed to really sit down and question my presuppositions. right,

130
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
I needed to say: have I really understood this at all?

131
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Have I really got a handle on this?

132
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:26,000
And so then those questions and just observing what the scouts in my troop were doing

133
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:32,000
helped me to kind of get over the fact that perhaps I had envisioned

134
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
something that was not only impossible,

135
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,000
it was something that was not going to actually achieve the aims that I thought it would.

136
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So that's kind of disillusioning, isn't it?

137
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
But disillusionment if you look at the word right,

138
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
You get rid of the illusions,

139
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
And every once in a while you got to sit down and you got to go: oh wow,

140
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,000
I'm trying to achieve something that is perhaps an illusion.

141
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:57,000
Maybe I should get real about this,

142
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
Maybe I should be more pragmatic, and that will help.

143
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,000
And so that begins by just sitting and observing and seeing what's going on

144
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
and trying to reconcile all these various things in your head and thinking about:

145
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
well, this is the direction I've always been headed in.

146
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:14,000
Maybe I'm right,

147
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Maybe I'm wrong here, at least introducing the fact that maybe things need to be changed,

148
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Yes,

149
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,000
And then moving on to build a more substantial vision

150
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:30,000
or picture of that future state that you're going to be in

151
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Now. what I found out, though, about scouting and about my work as a scouter?

152
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
that it wasn't really about following my vision at all.

153
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:46,000
It was about helping scouts form a vision for themselves and then make that vision a reality.

154
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:54,000
My work as a scouter then became simply letting the scouts realize their own vision

155
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
and abilities and the vast stores of potential in every young person.

156
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Scouting is designed to respond to the natural enthusiasm young people have

157
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:08,000
for the outdoors, their curiosity, their desire for achievement and recognition,

158
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:12,000
the strong drive for social membership and acceptance.

159
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
I mean young people have a natural propensity for cooperation and for leadership,

160
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:21,000
And if we accept that they come to us with these powerful characteristics in place,

161
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:27,000
then we simply maintain an atmosphere that encourages all the positive expression of these qualities

162
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:32,000
And we guard against influences that might quash their natural enthusiasm.

163
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:39,000
And in doing that, we begin to get them to form a vision of their future state,

164
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:40,000
a picture of themselves.

165
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Once I realized that things just made a lot more sense

166
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And it became easier to be a scouter and to become effective and to help young people. right,

167
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:55,000
And this is not to say that somehow we just abandon everything and we say:

168
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,000
well, scouts, what do you want to do now?

169
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Because what I want to do obviously doesn't work.

170
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,000
So what do you want to do?

171
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
No, no, this vision has some definition to it.

172
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:10,000
You lend definition and help them figure out that picture of their future state,

173
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,000
that vision,

174
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
And we have so many different tools to do that,

175
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:18,000
The strongest among them being the precepts and the ideals expressed in the Scout

176
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:19,000
Oath and Law right,

177
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
In whatever iteration. you happen to have it, wherever in the world you are,

178
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:28,000
we have a defined program of methods and approaches that we use. you know,

179
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:33,000
Scouts do what scouts do. as I'm fond of saying, we go camping and we go adventuring,

180
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:40,000
and we do that in small groups or patrols, and that's the form of the game of scouting that we play.

181
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
This isn't about a vacuum where we create something apart from those things.

182
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:44,000
No, no,

183
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:50,000
We use those things to help young people shape a picture of their future state,

184
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
a vision for themselves, and then we help them realize it.

185
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:58,000
So sit down and examine the picture that you have in your mind when you do your work as a scouter.

186
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
What picture is that?

187
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:01,000
What's your vision,

188
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:04,000
And is that driving you and making you frustrated,

189
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,000
Or is it leading you to help your scouts shape and realize their own vision?

190
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:14,000
Like I said, this was a pretty important thing for me as a scouter.

191
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Once I got that, I was much happier.

192
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
I think the Scouts were happier too.

193
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:51,000
E-mail, that is folks,

194
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:55,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

195
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:06,000
James sent me this email and said: congratulations on episode 300..

196
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,000
I'm looking forward to hearing more of your advice on episodes to come.

197
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:14,000
I'm a unit commissioner and I've heard something at a troop committee meeting

198
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
that I wanted to share with you and get your take on.

199
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
One of the adults said our courts of honor are poorly attended.

200
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:22,000
I'd like to propose we stop handing out rank badges at our troop meetings

201
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:27,000
and start requiring Scouts to attend a court of honor to get the badge.

202
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:30,000
We could still mention that they achieved something at the troop meeting,

203
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
but we'd make them get to the court of honor to get the badge.

204
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And James said: I went straight to the 2015 Guide to Advancement

205
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and it says- quote: when the Board of Review has approved his advancement,

206
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
a scout deserves recognition as soon as possible.

207
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
this should be done at a ceremony at the next unit meeting.

208
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:51,000
His achievement may be recognized again later during a formal court of honor.

209
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Unquote: The Guide to Advancement does not specifically say

210
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
when the scout should receive the badge,

211
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
so I guess it would be okay to hold the badge until the next court of honor.

212
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
But I always pause when I hear adults trying to put barriers in place

213
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:07,000
that require Scouts to do things we think they ought to be doing.

214
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:11,000
For example, one unit I work with requires Scouts to go on a camp out

215
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
to get a Scout Master Conference, which seems kind of forced.

216
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
I believe that if camp outs were fun and appealing.

217
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:21,000
adults wouldn't need to do things that require Scouts to attend.

218
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
The same would be true of a court of honor.

219
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
If courts of honor are of value to the scout,

220
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
then why do we need to require them to attend?

221
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:32,000
And if a Scout doesn't want to attend, why should we force them?

222
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
I'm curious to hear your take on this.

223
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
I'm hoping that I'm on the right track

224
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:40,000
and that my Scout Master CG training is paying off

225
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:44,000
Well, James, I agree with your assessment of both of these ideas

226
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
being forced on Scouts.

227
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
How is it that poor attendance at a court of honor

228
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
or a camp out is a Scout's fault?

229
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
I mean, I can imagine ways that it could be,

230
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
but is that really where we want to start?

231
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
I think it's poor manners and bad business

232
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
to blame the customer, isn't it?

233
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
I mean, if they don't like your product,

234
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
shouldn't you begin by asking what's wrong with the product

235
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
rather than what's wrong with the customer

236
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
If your sales are bad, you can't just decide that you're above the law

237
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
and flaunt the rules and regulations

238
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and do anything possible to compel customers to buy your product.

239
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
I mean, that wouldn't be very Scout-like, would it?

240
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:17,000
And I would argue this:

241
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
The Guide to Advancement certainly does specify

242
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
when a Scout gets the badge.

243
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
I mean it's just playing its day.

244
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:26,000
The Scout deserves recognition as soon as possible.

245
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:30,000
This should be done at a ceremony at the next unit meeting.

246
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
Speaking from my troop,

247
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
we have quarterly Courts of Honor. in place of a troop meeting

248
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
We have a parents meeting just prior to the court of honor.

249
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Anyone who thinks they'll get good attendance

250
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,000
by scheduling a court of honor in addition to troop meetings

251
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
is not really keeping up with the times. I don't think,

252
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
And you know we have a great time.

253
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Everybody shows up because they were going to show up that night anyway,

254
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:55,000
And we're having cookies and punch within an hour or so after we begin.

255
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
So we keep it short, we respect people's time

256
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and we make it easy to be there.

257
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
That's not very difficult to do, right.

258
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,000
And as for requiring Scouts to attend a camp out

259
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
to get a Scout Master Conference,

260
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
you know that's just another example

261
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
of these kind of coercive things

262
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
that we think we have to do to force Scouts to do something

263
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:15,000
because we're disappointed that they don't go on our campouts

264
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
or come to our Courts of Honor and things like that.

265
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
I get that. I get it

266
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:22,000
If you want to coerce your Scouts and force them into doing things.

267
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
I can't stop you from doing that.

268
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:27,000
But what I would suggest- and I think James would join me in suggesting-

269
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,000
is that you know, instead of trying to force people to do things,

270
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:34,000
how about looking at the things that you're asking them to do

271
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
and see if they're really reasonable?

272
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
James replied to that answer and he said:

273
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
sounds good. I'm glad we're on the same page with this one.

274
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
I'll admit that years ago I was one of those

275
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
forced the Scouts to do what we want them to do, Scouters.

276
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
But a couple of years of listening to your good advice

277
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
and I've come to see the error of my ways.

278
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:53,000
keep up the good work.

279
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
I especially like the business customer analogy in this case.

280
00:17:56,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Well, James, everybody tries and does what seems reasonable to them,

281
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and then you maybe discover that what you're doing isn't reasonable,

282
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
And so you know, hopefully you change,

283
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
because just keeping on doing unreasonable things

284
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
is kind of a frustrating way to be, isn't it?

285
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And the business customer analogy works up to a point,

286
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,000
like all analogies. all analogies just kind of break down at some point,

287
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:21,000
And so I offer it here with that in mind.

288
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
I mean, don't go crazy calling Scouts customers

289
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and the troop of business.

290
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Sometimes that analogy works and sometimes it doesn't,

291
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
So you know, let's not try and break it.

292
